"""Run the MoonBit port on data generated by the real upstream Python example.

WHAT THIS IS, AND WHY IT IS NOT LIKE THE OTHER 24 CHECKS
========================================================
Every other `validate_*_with_python.py` builds a reference and compares the
port against it. Those references are OURS. This one is not: the dataset
here comes from `doubleml.did.datasets.make_did_SZ2020`, called with
upstream's own seed and fed to upstream's own `DoubleMLDID` -- the exact
sequence in the `DoubleMLDID` docstring. So this asks a strictly harder
question than the rest: not "does the port agree with our second
implementation" but "does the port agree with upstream, on upstream's data".

WHY IT HAS TO LOOK LIKE THIS
============================
MoonBit 0.1.20261012 has no file I/O. Measured, not assumed: `@std/env`,
`@std/fs`, `@std/io` and `@std/process` all fail to resolve (error 3001) and
the builtin `read_file` does not exist (error 4021). A dataset cannot be
handed to the port as a file; it has to arrive as literals in a compiled
`.mbt` source.

So: write the generated dataset into `examples/parity/pyexample_data.mbt`,
run the emitter, compare, restore. The placeholder is committed because CI
runs `moon build` and `moon test` at the workspace root on a checkout where
this script has never run, and `main.mbt` references the fixture's symbols in
every build.

THE TOLERANCES ARE MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN
========================================
Upstream draws folds from numpy's global RNG; the port draws its own from
`seed=`. Those are different partitions of the same rows, so bit equality is
impossible and the tolerance has to come from the fold noise itself. Fitting
upstream under 20 different fold seeds (`_verify/_se_noise_1660.py`):

    coef  upstream over 20 seeds: min 0.019404  max 0.157364  sd 0.034670
    se    upstream over 20 seeds: min 0.140516  max 0.151343  sd 0.002994

An earlier revision of this file read an 8% SE gap there as an OPEN PORT
DEFECT. It was not a port defect -- it was this fixture misconfiguring the
port. Upstream defaults to `in_sample_normalization=True` and refuses an
`ml_m` without `.predict_proba()`; the port defaults to `false` and a linear
regression. Four-way measurement on the generated dataset:

    ml_m=reg  norm=False -> se 0.0756218   <- the port's defaults
    ml_m=reg  norm=True  -> se 0.0742462
    ml_m=cls  norm=False -> se 0.0720907
    ml_m=cls  norm=True  -> se 0.0708030   <- upstream's configuration
    upstream              -> se 0.0700322

94% of the gap was the fixture. With upstream's actual configuration the port
lands 0.03 sd from upstream on the coefficient and 0.89 sd on the standard
error, so BOTH rows are now hard 3-sd gates and the pinned-ratio row is gone.
`main.mbt`'s `sec_did_pyexample` carries the same measurement next to the
settings that produce it.

THE CS2021 HALF LIVES NEXT DOOR, AND THE REASON IT WAS SKIPPED WAS WRONG
========================================================================
This file covers `DoubleMLDID` (single cohort, SZ2020). The multi-cohort half
is `pyexample_did_cs2021.py`, on upstream's `DoubleMLDIDMulti`. It did not
exist when this note was first written, and the two reasons given for skipping
it were both measured FALSE:

  * "Upstream ships the CS2021 DGP but no CS2021 estimator." Upstream ships
    `DoubleMLDIDMulti`. It is in `doubleml.did.__all__` and its own docstring
    example is built on `make_did_CS2021`.
  * "The never-treated conventions disagree, so the port treats a cohort as its
    own control." They agree for INTEGER `g`. `_get_never_treated_value` is
    dtype-dependent -- `0` for integer, `inf` for floating, `NaT` for datetime
    -- and `0` is exactly this port's rule (`discover_groups_times`,
    `did_cs.mbt:89-95`). The `inf` that broke the attempt came from
    `time_type="float"`; recoding to integer reconciles them.

Both claims were refuted by running the thing rather than reading the module
list. A refusal to build something should never rest on a claim that a five-line
probe would have settled.

USAGE
=====
    python pyexample_did.py --check     # the gate: write, run, compare, restore
    python pyexample_did.py --write     # write the fixture and leave it
    python pyexample_did.py --restore   # put the placeholder back
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import io
import os
import subprocess
import sys

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
FIXTURE = os.path.join(ROOT, "examples", "parity", "pyexample_data.mbt")

# `parity_helper` lives beside this file; `moon` must be on PATH. Same two
# lines every `validate_*_with_python.py` uses.
sys.path.insert(0, ROOT)

# Two seeds, and they are not interchangeable.
#
# DGP_SEED / N_OBS choose the DATASET. They were chosen by sweeping
# (`_verify/_seed_sweep_1660.py`) for a fixture where the treatment effect
# actually clears the fold-assignment noise, because the obvious choice is
# worthless:
#
#     n_obs  dgp_seed  coef      fold_sd    |coef|/sd
#       500        42  +0.06321   0.04150      1.52   <- upstream's own
#       500         7  -0.18557   0.06531      2.84
#     2000        42  +0.02540   0.01281      1.98
#     2000         7  -0.15018   0.01128     13.31   <- used here
#
# At 500/42 the effect is indistinguishable from noise: PERMUTING the
# treatment column moves the coefficient by 0.0007, so the estimate is 0.063
# of pure sampling noise. A gate built on that passes whether the estimator
# is correct or hard-codes zero. At 2000/7 the signal is 13x the noise and
# the gate can actually bite.
DGP_SEED, N_OBS = 7, 2000

# FIT_SEED is upstream's own (np.random.seed(42) in the docstring example) and
# only sets which folds upstream draws.
FIT_SEED = 42

# Deterministic learners in place of upstream's RandomForests: a forest would
# make the reference depend on a second RNG, and the point is that the DATA
# and the ESTIMATOR are upstream's, not that they are stochastic.
N_FOLDS, N_REP = 2, 1

# --- tolerances, all measured (see the module docstring) --------------------
COEF_SD = 0.01128          # upstream coef, sd over 10 fold seeds at 2000/7
COEF_TOL = 3 * COEF_SD      # = 0.03384
SE_SD = 0.000865          # upstream se, sd over 10 fold seeds at 2000/7
SE_TOL = 3 * SE_SD        # = 0.002595


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# data + reference
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def build_sz2020():
    """The upstream example, end to end. Returns the data and the reference."""
    import numpy as np
    from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression, LogisticRegression
    import doubleml as dml
    from doubleml.did.datasets import make_did_SZ2020

    np.random.seed(DGP_SEED)
    df = make_did_SZ2020(n_obs=N_OBS, return_type="DataFrame")
    x = np.column_stack([df["Z%d" % k].to_numpy() for k in (1, 2, 3, 4)])
    y = df["y"].to_numpy(dtype=float)
    d = df["d"].to_numpy(dtype=float)

    obj = dml.DoubleMLDIDData(df, "y", "d")
    np.random.seed(FIT_SEED)
    model = dml.DoubleMLDID(obj, LinearRegression(), LogisticRegression(),
                            n_folds=N_FOLDS, n_rep=N_REP)
    model.fit()
    return {"x": x, "y": y, "d": d,
            "coef": float(np.asarray(model.coef).ravel()[0]),
            "se": float(np.asarray(model.se).ravel()[0])}


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# fixture emission
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def _fmt(values, render, per_line):
    out, line = [], []
    for v in values:
        line.append(render(v))
        if len(line) == per_line:
            out.append("    " + ", ".join(line) + ",")
            line = []
    if line:
        out.append("    " + ", ".join(line) + ",")
    return "\n".join(out)


def fmt_doubles(values, per_line=6):
    """Round-trippable: repr() of a float is exact in Python 3."""
    return _fmt(values, lambda v: repr(float(v)), per_line)


HEADER = """// GENERATED BY pyexample_did.py -- DO NOT EDIT, DO NOT COMMIT.
// Placeholder is restored by `python pyexample_did.py --restore`.

fn pyexample_generated() -> Int32 {
  1
}

fn sz2020_n_obs() -> Int32 {
  %d
}

fn sz2020_n_feat() -> Int32 {
  %d
}

fn sz2020_x() -> Array[Double] {
  [
%s
  ]
}

fn sz2020_y() -> Array[Double] {
  [
%s
  ]
}

fn sz2020_d() -> Array[Double] {
  [
%s
  ]
}
"""


# The anchor of the CS2021 half. Both gates write the SAME fixture file and
# splice DISJOINT halves around this line, because one MoonBit build serves both
# and a second placeholder would need a second symbol set for no benefit.
#
# This was measured the hard way: this writer used to emit the whole file from
# `HEADER`, which silently truncated the `cs2021_*` block that the other gate
# owns. `main.mbt` still referenced those symbols, so `moon run` died with
# Error 4021 (undefined identifier) -- and the failure pointed at `main.mbt`,
# which is nowhere near the file that was actually wrong.
CS2021_ANCHOR = "\nfn cs2021_generated() -> Int32 {"


def write_fixture(sz):
    """Replace the SZ2020 half only; leave the CS2021 half byte-identical."""
    with io.open(FIXTURE, "r", encoding="utf-8", newline="") as fh:
        text = fh.read()
    _head, sep, tail = text.partition(CS2021_ANCHOR)
    if not sep:
        raise SystemExit("could not find the cs2021 anchor in %s" % FIXTURE)
    body = HEADER % (sz["x"].shape[0], sz["x"].shape[1],
                     fmt_doubles(sz["x"].ravel()),
                     fmt_doubles(sz["y"]),
                     fmt_doubles(sz["d"]))
    out = body + sep + tail
    io.open(FIXTURE, "w", encoding="utf-8", newline="\n").write(out)
    return len(out)


def restore_fixture():
    """Restore via git. Only for the standalone `--restore` mode."""
    rel = os.path.relpath(FIXTURE, ROOT)
    if not os.path.isfile(FIXTURE):
        raise SystemExit("fixture missing; restore with: git checkout -- " + rel)
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "checkout", "--", rel], cwd=ROOT, capture_output=True)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise SystemExit("could not restore the placeholder via git (is it "
                         "committed?): %s" % (r.stderr or b"").decode("utf-8", "replace"))


def snapshot_fixture():
    with open(FIXTURE, "rb") as fh:
        return fh.read()


def restore_fixture_bytes(blob):
    """Restore from bytes, with no dependency on git.

    This is what `--check` uses, and the difference is not cosmetic: the CI
    simulator materialises the tree with `git archive`, which contains no
    `.git` directory at all, so a git-based restore cannot work there. The
    first version of this gate used git and the simulator failed it in 8.7s
    with an exit code that said nothing about why. The in-memory snapshot is
    also the honest dependency: the gate's contract is "put back what you
    found", not "ask a version control system for it".
    """
    with open(FIXTURE, "wb") as fh:
        fh.write(blob)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# gate
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def run_emitter():
    import parity_helper as ph
    return ph.parse(ph.run_parity())["did_pyexample"]


def main(argv):
    mode = argv[1] if len(argv) > 1 else "--check"
    if mode == "--restore":
        restore_fixture()
        print("restored the committed placeholder")
        return 0
    if mode not in ("--check", "--write"):
        print(__doc__)
        return 2

    sz = build_sz2020()
    print("upstream example regenerated")
    print("  make_did_SZ2020(dgp_seed=%d, n_obs=%d) -> x%s y%s d%s"
          % (DGP_SEED, N_OBS, sz["x"].shape, sz["y"].shape, sz["d"].shape))
    print("  upstream DoubleMLDID -> coef %.17g   se %.17g"
          % (sz["coef"], sz["se"]))
    print()

    # Snapshot BEFORE writing. Restoring from these bytes rather than from git
    # is what makes this work in a `git archive` tree, which has no .git.
    snapshot = snapshot_fixture()
    nbytes = write_fixture(sz)
    if mode == "--write":
        print("wrote %d bytes to %s (leave with --restore)"
              % (nbytes, os.path.relpath(FIXTURE, ROOT)))
        return 0

    try:
        sec = run_emitter()
    finally:
        restore_fixture_bytes(snapshot)

    if sec["val"].get("sz_generated") != 1.0:
        print("FAIL: the emitter did not see the generated fixture")
        return 1

    import parity_helper as ph
    coef = sec["val"]["sz_coef"]
    se = sec["val"]["sz_se"]
    rows = [
        # HARD GATE. The effect is -0.150 and the band is 0.034, so this row
        # bites: a corrupted panel cannot pass it.
        ("sz_coef vs upstream (3sd=%.6f)" % COEF_TOL, coef, sz["coef"],
         abs(coef - sz["coef"]) <= COEF_TOL),
        # Also a hard gate now. This row used to be a pinned-ratio excuse for
        # an "8% SE defect" that turned out to be THIS FIXTURE misconfiguring
        # the port: upstream defaults to in_sample_normalization=True and
        # refuses an ml_m without .predict_proba(), while the port defaults to
        # false and a linear regression. With upstream's actual configuration
        # the two SEs agree to 0.00077, which is 0.89 fold-sd -- inside the
        # noise. See the four-way measurement in main.mbt's docstring.
        ("sz_se vs upstream (3sd=%.6f)" % SE_TOL, se, sz["se"],
         abs(se - sz["se"]) <= SE_TOL),
        ("sz_n_obs", sec["val"]["sz_n_obs"], float(sz["y"].shape[0]),
         sec["val"]["sz_n_obs"] == float(sz["y"].shape[0])),
    ]
    print("fold-noise band, measured over 10 upstream fold seeds:")
    print("  coef: sd %.6f  ->  port is %.2f sd from upstream"
          % (COEF_SD, abs(coef - sz["coef"]) / COEF_SD))
    print("  se  : sd %.6f  ->  port is %.2f sd from upstream"
          % (SE_SD, abs(se - sz["se"]) / SE_SD))
    print()

    rc = ph.report("pyexample: port vs upstream make_did_SZ2020", rows, ok=True)
    # The fixture must be back to the placeholder even on failure. This is a
    # byte comparison, not a git-status string: right CRLF, BOM or trailing
    # newline would still be a dirty tree, and this repo has been bitten by
    # exactly that. A git-free tree (the CI simulator materialises one with
    # `git archive`) has no status to ask about, so the bytes are the only
    # honest test.
    with open(FIXTURE, "rb") as fh:
        after = fh.read()
    same = after == snapshot
    print()
    print("fixture restored byte for byte: %s" % same)
    return rc if same else 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
